1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:03,324
A server in a rack has no screen and no mouse.

2
00:00:03,344 --> 00:00:07,612
Yet by default it may start a full graphical desktop at every boot,

3
00:00:07,632 --> 00:00:12,092
and waste memory on it. One command decides what a machine boots into.

4
00:00:12,562 --> 00:00:17,299
In this video: configure systems to boot into a specific target automatically.

5
00:00:21,819 --> 00:00:24,402
Here's the task, the way an exam would state it.

6
00:00:24,872 --> 00:00:28,670
Configure systems to boot into a specific target automatically.

7
00:00:29,140 --> 00:00:32,982
We'll read the default target, change it, and prove it after a reboot.

8
00:00:39,502 --> 00:00:42,566
A target is a goal that system D boots toward.

9
00:00:42,586 --> 00:00:46,722
It groups the services needed for that state. Two matter here.

10
00:00:46,742 --> 00:00:51,884
Multi dash user dot target is a full text system, with networking and logins,

11
00:00:51,904 --> 00:00:56,911
but no graphical desktop. Graphical dot target is the same, plus a desktop.

12
00:01:03,381 --> 00:01:07,217
The default target is the one a machine reaches at every boot.

13
00:01:07,237 --> 00:01:09,953
It is just a link, named default dot target.

14
00:01:15,423 --> 00:01:20,137
Before the demo, silently, the lab sets the default to graphical dot target,

15
00:01:20,157 --> 00:01:23,937
so we have something to change. This step changes the system.

16
00:01:32,457 --> 00:01:37,954
First, read it. Systemctl get dash default prints the default target. Here:

17
00:01:37,974 --> 00:01:39,498
graphical dot target.

18
00:01:48,018 --> 00:01:53,681
Now change it. Sudo systemctl set dash default, multi dash user dot target.

19
00:01:53,701 --> 00:01:57,167
It removes the old link and creates a new one.

20
00:01:57,187 --> 00:02:00,350
Get dash default now says multi dash user.

21
00:02:08,820 --> 00:02:11,297
And ls dash l shows the link itself:

22
00:02:11,317 --> 00:02:15,182
default dot target points to multi dash user dot target.

23
00:02:15,202 --> 00:02:18,997
Set dash default does nothing more than move this link.

24
00:02:27,517 --> 00:02:30,953
Our five steps: task, demo, check, reboot, and your turn.

25
00:02:32,473 --> 00:02:38,128
We reboot. Get dash default still says multi dash user. Multi dash user is active.

26
00:02:38,148 --> 00:02:39,927
And graphical is inactive:

27
00:02:39,947 --> 00:02:44,080
the machine booted into the target we chose, and no desktop.

28
00:02:54,600 --> 00:02:57,902
Your turn. The lab has set the default to graphical.

29
00:02:57,922 --> 00:03:02,564
Make multi dash user dot target the default, so it applies at every boot.

30
00:03:02,584 --> 00:03:05,503
Run the checker, then reboot and run it again.

31
00:03:12,973 --> 00:03:14,723
Try before you open the solution.

32
00:03:20,243 --> 00:03:22,804
Next up: configure time service clients.

33
00:03:23,274 --> 00:03:26,177
Subscribe to follow the series, and let's keep going.
